# 3. Contractor Discount Management

## 3.1 Discount Policy

Admin must be able to create multiple discount policies.

Each policy should contain:

- Policy Name
- Policy Code
- Description
- Discount Type
- Discount Value
- Eligibility Rules
- Applicable Estimate Components
- Minimum Estimate Amount
- Maximum Discount Amount
- Effective Start Date
- Effective End Date
- Usage Limit, if applicable
- Status
- Created By
- Updated By
- Created At
- Updated At

Example policy:

Policy Name: Approved Contractor Discount  
Policy Code: CONTRACTOR-10  
Discount Type: PERCENTAGE  
Discount Value: 10  
Status: ACTIVE

This is an illustrative example only. It is not an approved commercial value.

## 3.2 Supported Discount Types

The system must support two discount types.

### A. Percentage Discount

Formula:

Discount Amount = Eligible Amount × Discount Percentage / 100

Example:

Eligible Estimate Amount = $2,500  
Discount Percentage = 10%

Discount Amount = $250

Final Amount = $2,250

### B. Fixed Amount Discount

Formula:

Discount Amount = Minimum of:

- Configured Fixed Discount
- Eligible Estimate Amount

Example:

Eligible Estimate Amount = $2,500  
Configured Fixed Discount = $200

Discount Amount = $200

Final Amount = $2,300

All numerical values above are examples.

## 3.3 Discount Applicability

Admin must control which estimate components are eligible.

Possible applicability options:

- Entire eligible estimate
- Materials only
- Services only
- Selected material categories
- Selected services
- Other explicitly configured estimate components

The implementation must use the existing Phase 6 estimate breakdown.

Do not apply discounts to components that are not explicitly eligible.

The same amount must not receive multiple discounts unintentionally.

If multiple policies can apply, Admin must configure the stacking rule. Until a stacking policy is approved, only one discount policy should be applied to an estimate.

## 3.4 Contractor Eligibility

A discount policy must support configurable eligibility rules.

Possible conditions:

- Contractor account is approved.
- Contractor account is active.
- Contractor belongs to an eligible contractor category or group.
- Contractor has a valid policy assignment.
- Contractor satisfies minimum business requirements configured by Admin.
- Contractor is within the policy's effective dates.
- Contractor has not exceeded applicable usage limits.

Important:

Only approved and active contractors should receive contractor discounts.

The backend must validate eligibility every time a discount is applied.

Never trust the frontend to decide whether a contractor qualifies.

## 3.5 Discount Limits

Admin should be able to configure:

- Minimum eligible estimate amount.
- Maximum discount amount.
- Maximum discount percentage, if required.
- Per-estimate usage.
- Per-contractor usage limit.
- Total policy usage limit.
- Policy effective dates.

All limits are optional unless enabled by Admin.

If a limit is not configured, the application must not invent one.

## 3.6 Discount Policy Lifecycle

Recommended policy statuses:

- DRAFT
- ACTIVE
- INACTIVE
- EXPIRED
- ARCHIVED

Expected behavior:

DRAFT:
Policy is being configured and cannot be applied.

ACTIVE:
Policy is eligible for application when all conditions pass.

INACTIVE:
Policy cannot be applied.

EXPIRED:
Policy has passed its configured end date.

ARCHIVED:
Policy is retained for historical reference and cannot be applied.

The backend must enforce valid status transitions.

